TI  - Synergistic enhancement of biomethanation integrating H 2 injection with external CO 2 in thermophilic anaerobic digestion of sewage sludge.
JO  - Bioresource technology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.biortech.2025.133821
AB  - Integrating power-to-gas technology with anaerobic digestion offers a sustainable route for enhancing biogas production through H 2 supplementation. This study aimed to develop an energy-efficient biomethanation strategy by identifying the operational limitations of thermophilic anaerobic digestion of sewage sludge, where maintaining stable performance remains a key challenge. Efficient gas-liquid mass transfer enabled minimal residual H 2 (1.4 ± 0.8 %), resulting in a high CH 4 content (86 ± 3 %) and stable biogas yield (0.65 ± 0.09 NL g-VS-added -1 ). Sudden H 2 overloading reduced the CO 2 levels and increased the pH, thereby triggering process inhibition. Recovery occurred instantly upon pH restoration and CO 2 addition, likely due to the metabolic versatility and adaptive capacity of the microbial community. The dominance of bacteria within the orders Thermoanaerobacterales and Clostridiales, alongside hydrogenotrophic methanogens, efficiently directed H 2 toward CH 4 formation without compromising overall process stability. Importantly, CO 2 addition stabilized the pH and enhanced methanation, underscoring its role in both process control and carbon capture. The H 2 S concentration in the H 2 -added reactor was minimal, ranging from undetectable to 25 ppm. These findings offer critical insights into H 2 -assisted thermophilic digestion of sewage sludge and demonstrate that external CO 2 addition supports stable biomethanation while enhancing the sustainability of the system.
